## Instructions

1. Decide which repositories this story touches. Read the relevant specs and code in the clones —
   plans grounded in real code beat plans from descriptions. A story touching a single repo is fine:
   the plan is then a single issue.
2. For each repository, draft one issue: target project, title, and body.

Issue-body rules (smithy sees ONLY its target repo):
- Write bodies high-level: goal, expected behavior/contract, acceptance criteria. Smithy plans the
  exact files itself.
- Spell out cross-component touchpoints explicitly: API shapes, endpoint paths, queue/message names,
  config keys. Sibling agents cannot see each other's repos.
- NO sequencing notes in bodies ("after X merges") — ordering is expressed only via dependsOn.
- At most {{ max_issues }} issues.
3. Express ordering as dependsOn: a list of ZERO-BASED indexes into your issues array. An issue is
   only handed to a smithy agent once all its dependencies' merge requests are merged.
4. List open questions only when something genuinely blocks planning — prefer a reasonable assumption
   stated in the summary.

Return the plan as structured output: summary (2-4 sentences on the approach and how the pieces fit),
issues (project, title, body, dependsOn), openQuestions.
